comments.body:
Thank you for providing examples. The level selection is a good example. Let's work with that. Assuming you're using a state for the level, changing it to the same level currently is a no-op, which is what motivates an identity state transition. But I believe there's another verb here, which is you also want to reset the level, which can happen to and in any level at any time, which is not a state; it's an event.

Now you may object that resetting a level is tearing it down and recreating it. But for some games, like sokoban for instance, resetting a level can just be resetting a few transforms to their original positions. Resetting can be much cheaper than tearing down and setting back up. Here's my pseudo-code to facilitate the discussion.

```rust
#[derive(States)]
pub enum GameState {
    Level(pub u8),
}
// ...
        .add_systems(OnExit(GameState::Level), teardown_level)
        .add_systems(OnEnter(GameState::Level), setup_level)

// Let's say there are two ways to deal with level resets.
// 1. Brute force approach. (Expensive)
        .add_systems(Update, (teardown_level, setup_level).chain().run_if(on_event(ResetLevel)))

// 2. Minimal dynamic state. (Cheap)
        .add_systems(Update, reset_player_and_elements.run_if(on_event(ResetLevel)))
```

For me this reinforces my point. A state machine is a good fundamental building block, but it is in its own way opinionated about what constitutes a state: You can only be in one state, and you can only transition to another state. Often times trying to use a state machine forces you to recon with latent or implicit states; this is good! It helps your design become more explicit.

Perhaps we should consider creating an example that shows this kind of level selection pattern using states and a reset event. I notice there aren't any examples with states that have additional data to them like `Level(pub u8)`.

You can have them if you want them, but I still think it's not worth doing because you're taking a state machine and asking it to treat some state, say, `Menu` as both `MenuFresh` and `MenuDirty` when what's preferable is to let the state machine help you. If something makes your menu dirty such that it requires a refresh then that's something you should capture in your states. Then you can write your code that deals with that explicit state instead of transitioning to the same state you're already in and doing a bunch of work that is sometimes required (when it's dirty) and sometimes not (when it's not dirty). 

Every same-state transition that requires an "operation" is a latent, unexplicated state waiting to be exposed. Expose it. 

```rust
/// ```ignore
/// app.add_system(Update, refresh_mesh.run_if(on_any_enter(AppState::Menu)));
/// ```
pub fn on_any_enter<S: States>(state: S) -> impl Fn(EventReader<StateTransitionEvent<S>>) -> bool {
    move |mut reader: EventReader<StateTransitionEvent<S>>| {
        reader.read().any(|e| e.entered.as_ref().map(|enter| *enter == state).unwrap_or(false))
    }
}

/// ```ignore
/// app.add_system(Update, teardown_mesh.run_if(on_any_exit(AppState::Menu)));
/// ```
pub fn on_any_exit<S: States>(state: S) -> impl Fn(EventReader<StateTransitionEvent<S>>) -> bool {
    move |mut reader: EventReader<StateTransitionEvent<S>>| {
        reader.read().any(|e| e.exited.as_ref().map(|exit| *exit == state).unwrap_or(false))
    }
}
```

Thank you. That's helpful. And that's cool that you're supporting multiple languages. Perhaps I'm being too stackoverflow-y about state transitions. You should be able to do it how you want to do it.

If you want to do it via same-state transitions, you can do it this way with the above code.

```rust
// OLD
// app.add_systems(OnEnter(AppState::Menu), update_menu);
// NEW
app.add_systems(Update, update_menu.run_if(on_any_enter(AppState::Menu));
```
